WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:127826

1
00:00:00.133 --> 00:00:01.434
In this lesson, we'll show you

2
00:00:01.434 --> 00:00:05.238
how to create and interact with MemGPT agents
using the Letta framework.

3
00:00:05.705 --> 00:00:08.508
We'll also go over how to understand
the agent state, such

4
00:00:08.508 --> 00:00:11.511
as the system prompt, tools
and the memory of the agent.

5
00:00:11.745 --> 00:00:14.814
We'll also learn how to view and edit
the agent archival memory.

6
00:00:15.181 --> 00:00:17.050
All right, let's go.

7
00:00:17.050 --> 00:00:21.054
You can use Letta to create
MemGPT agents. MemGPT agents are stateful

8
00:00:21.054 --> 00:00:24.057
and have explicitly managed sections
of their context window.

9
00:00:24.224 --> 00:00:27.227
In this lesson, we'll go over
a different parts of the agent state

10
00:00:27.427 --> 00:00:30.196
and also archival memory
and recall memory.

11
00:00:30.196 --> 00:00:32.499
We'll also go over how you can design
agents.

12
00:00:32.499 --> 00:00:35.769
So, agents can be designed
by controlling these following knobs.

13
00:00:35.935 --> 00:00:38.505
So the prompt which includes things
like the system prompt

14
00:00:38.505 --> 00:00:41.174
and the persona
that defines the agent's behavior,

15
00:00:41.174 --> 00:00:45.178
the agent's tools, the way the agent
manages and organizes its memory,

16
00:00:45.678 --> 00:00:49.649
and also the content of the agent's
memory, both inside of core and archival.

17
00:00:50.050 --> 00:00:52.685
These knobs define
what's placed into the LLM context

18
00:00:52.685 --> 00:00:55.688
at each step, thereby
defining the agent's behavior.

19
00:00:55.855 --> 00:00:58.725
So, I'm first going to create a Letta client.

20
00:00:58.725 --> 00:01:02.462
So, I can import the Letta client
from the Letta client package.

21
00:01:02.695 --> 00:01:05.298
And this will basically connect
my client to the running

22
00:01:05.298 --> 00:01:09.436
Letta service a local host
8283. Letta is a bit different

23
00:01:09.436 --> 00:01:12.806
from other agent frameworks in the sense
that it runs the service

24
00:01:13.106 --> 00:01:16.042
so you connect to a running
Letta server and it's by interacting

25
00:01:16.042 --> 00:01:19.045
with that server that you create agents
and interact with them.

26
00:01:19.379 --> 00:01:23.116
You already have a running Letta server
on your notebook sandbox environment.

27
00:01:23.383 --> 00:01:25.118
And so you don't need to worry
about starting this up.

28
00:01:25.118 --> 00:01:27.220
But if you're using Letta yourself

29
00:01:27.220 --> 00:01:27.821
outside of this

30
00:01:27.821 --> 00:01:30.190
course, you should make sure
to start a Letta service.

31
00:01:30.190 --> 00:01:34.327
Or to either connect to a Letta cloud
with an API key or a Letta desktop.

32
00:01:35.095 --> 00:01:37.363
So I'm going to first create this client.

33
00:01:37.363 --> 00:01:39.933
And then I'm also going to initialize
a helper tool.

34
00:01:39.933 --> 00:01:42.502
So, a tool to print a message
in a nice way.

35
00:01:42.502 --> 00:01:45.071
So, when we get responses back
from Letta agents

36
00:01:45.071 --> 00:01:46.973
they're going to return a message type
that

37
00:01:46.973 --> 00:01:49.109
has a couple of different variants.

38
00:01:49.109 --> 00:01:50.777
So, one is a reasoning message

39
00:01:50.777 --> 00:01:52.245
generated by the agent.

40
00:01:52.245 --> 00:01:53.813
Another is an assistant message.

41
00:01:53.813 --> 00:01:56.449
So this is agents communication
that we're getting back.

42
00:01:56.449 --> 00:01:59.452
There's also tool calls. And tool returns.

43
00:01:59.586 --> 00:02:02.655
Tool returns are the return result
of any tool execution.

44
00:02:02.655 --> 00:02:05.725
This is optional
but often contains important information.

45
00:02:06.092 --> 00:02:08.027
And then there's of course
also user message.

46
00:02:08.027 --> 00:02:11.131
So this is just, any kind of message
that we send to the agent.

47
00:02:12.298 --> 00:02:13.800
So, Letta basically allows

48
00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:17.203
you to create persistent LLM agents
that have memory built in.

49
00:02:17.604 --> 00:02:20.240
So, the Letta service will actually save
all the state

50
00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:21.875
related to an agent into a database.

51
00:02:21.875 --> 00:02:24.744
So you don't need to worry
about checkpointing or reloading an agent.

52
00:02:24.744 --> 00:02:26.613
That state will always be there.

53
00:02:26.613 --> 00:02:29.916
So to start we're going to just create
a really simple agent using Letta.

54
00:02:30.850 --> 00:02:31.584
We can do this by

55
00:02:31.584 --> 00:02:35.722
basically calling the agent creation
function on the Letta client.

56
00:02:36.022 --> 00:02:37.056
So we pass

57
00:02:37.056 --> 00:02:38.424
in a couple fields into create.

58
00:02:38.424 --> 00:02:41.194
So one is the agent name.
So this is optional.

59
00:02:41.194 --> 00:02:43.329
But I'm just going to name
this simple agent.

60
00:02:43.329 --> 00:02:44.697
There's also the memory blocks

61
00:02:44.697 --> 00:02:46.266
that are stored in context.

62
00:02:46.266 --> 00:02:47.333
So I have two here.

63
00:02:47.333 --> 00:02:49.068
One is the human memory block.

64
00:02:49.068 --> 00:02:50.703
The other is the persona.

65
00:02:50.703 --> 00:02:53.640
And for the human I'm
actually overriding the character limit.

66
00:02:53.640 --> 00:02:56.776
So, I'm basically telling the
how much space of the context

67
00:02:56.776 --> 00:03:00.547
window can be allocated
to the human section of the memory block.

68
00:03:00.747 --> 00:03:03.750
So sometimes you'll see
that you might start running out of space.

69
00:03:04.050 --> 00:03:05.618
This is somewhat intentional.

70
00:03:05.618 --> 00:03:08.855
It's basically meant to kind of make sure
that we don't spend too much space

71
00:03:08.855 --> 00:03:11.925
on an in context memory,
but sometimes you'll want to allocate

72
00:03:11.925 --> 00:03:15.595
more than the default 5k character
limit to memory block.

73
00:03:16.129 --> 00:03:18.231
And they'll also specify the LLM model.

74
00:03:18.231 --> 00:03:20.767
So, GPT-4o-mini
and then also an embedding model.

75
00:03:22.035 --> 00:03:22.669
So, this will

76
00:03:22.669 --> 00:03:25.672
create an agent on the server
that we can now message.

77
00:03:26.539 --> 00:03:29.509
So to message agent
we can also do this through the client.

78
00:03:29.509 --> 00:03:31.311
Specifying the agent ID

79
00:03:31.311 --> 00:03:34.314
that we got back from the agent state
from the agent creation.

80
00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:36.449
And then we'll pass in a list of messages.

81
00:03:36.449 --> 00:03:40.186
So we'll just send in a user message
that just says: " how's it going".

82
00:03:40.887 --> 00:03:42.422
And this will give us back a response

83
00:03:42.422 --> 00:03:45.458
that includes a list of the messages,
that were generated by the server.

84
00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:49.796
So we can see here
that we got two messages back.

85
00:03:49.796 --> 00:03:51.464
So the first one was a reasoning message.

86
00:03:51.464 --> 00:03:53.600
Basically, the agent thinking to itself:

87
00:03:53.600 --> 00:03:55.034
"The user seems casual and friendly.

88
00:03:55.034 --> 00:03:56.502
Let's match the energy."

89
00:03:56.502 --> 00:03:59.172
And then the agent sends a message back
to us.

90
00:03:59.172 --> 00:04:01.541
Saying "hey there, I'm doing great.
Thanks for asking.

91
00:04:01.541 --> 00:04:03.076
How about you?"

92
00:04:03.076 --> 00:04:04.344
The response also contains

93
00:04:04.344 --> 00:04:07.447
usage information. So we can print things
out like the completion

94
00:04:07.447 --> 00:04:10.717
tokens, the prompt tokens,
and also the number of steps taken.

95
00:04:11.184 --> 00:04:13.720
So, the step count here
is how many times was

96
00:04:13.720 --> 00:04:16.723
the LLM invoked
or how many steps did the agent take.

97
00:04:16.956 --> 00:04:18.891
In this case
the agent just did one thing.

98
00:04:18.891 --> 00:04:21.761
It just message us back.
So the sub count is just one.

99
00:04:21.761 --> 00:04:25.064
But for more complex tasks where there's
a long sequence of tool calling,

100
00:04:25.331 --> 00:04:27.567
you'll see that the step
count can often be higher.

101
00:04:28.735 --> 00:04:31.971
So when we created
the agent, we got back an agent state.

102
00:04:31.971 --> 00:04:34.073
So this contains all the state associated

103
00:04:34.073 --> 00:04:36.075
with an agent,
which includes a couple different things.

104
00:04:36.075 --> 00:04:39.579
So, one is the system prompt
that defines the agent's behavior.

105
00:04:39.746 --> 00:04:42.081
There's also the memory, the memory blocks.

106
00:04:42.081 --> 00:04:43.149
There's also tools.

107
00:04:43.149 --> 00:04:44.984
So these are tools
that the agent can call.

108
00:04:44.984 --> 00:04:46.819
And then there's
also the memory of the agent.

109
00:04:46.819 --> 00:04:49.322
So we can actually observe
all of these things

110
00:04:49.322 --> 00:04:52.525
by either looking into the agent state
or calling more client tools.

111
00:04:53.393 --> 00:04:55.194
So, we can actually view the system

112
00:04:55.194 --> 00:04:58.097
prompt of the agent
by looking at agent status system.

113
00:04:58.097 --> 00:05:00.533
So this is fairly long.
It has a lot of details that are

114
00:05:00.533 --> 00:05:04.103
kind of trying to override the default
system prompts of the LLM providers.

115
00:05:04.404 --> 00:05:07.407
And then also a lot of information
about how memory should be managed.

116
00:05:07.407 --> 00:05:09.242
You can of course
edit this if you want to,

117
00:05:09.242 --> 00:05:11.844
but we generally recommend
being pretty careful with this,

118
00:05:11.844 --> 00:05:14.681
since it can affect
how memory management is done.

119
00:05:14.681 --> 00:05:17.717
And then we can also see
each of the tools that the agent calls.

120
00:05:18.017 --> 00:05:18.751
So this is

121
00:05:18.751 --> 00:05:21.754
agent looking at the tool names
inside of agency dot

122
00:05:21.754 --> 00:05:22.588
tools.

123
00:05:22.588 --> 00:05:24.324
So we can see that it has

124
00:05:24.324 --> 00:05:27.560
archival memory insert and archival memory
search to basically

125
00:05:27.660 --> 00:05:31.064
insert memories into external memory
and also search them.

126
00:05:31.464 --> 00:05:34.567
It can also search the conversation
history that it has.

127
00:05:34.567 --> 00:05:38.638
So not all the messages will be in context
if you have a really long conversation.

128
00:05:38.838 --> 00:05:41.974
So the agent has the ability
to search the conversational history.

129
00:05:42.442 --> 00:05:44.610
And there's also a core
memory append and replace.

130
00:05:44.610 --> 00:05:45.111
Which give the

131
00:05:45.111 --> 00:05:49.582
agent the ability to edit the in-context
memory blocks with new information.

132
00:05:50.016 --> 00:05:52.318
And then finally there's of course
the send message tool.

133
00:05:52.318 --> 00:05:54.053
So the agent messages

134
00:05:54.053 --> 00:05:57.623
are actually generated by the agent
calling send message explicitly.

135
00:05:58.124 --> 00:05:58.725
This allows the

136
00:05:58.725 --> 00:06:01.094
agent to understand that it's making an

137
00:06:01.094 --> 00:06:03.963
explicit choice
to communicate back to the user.

138
00:06:03.963 --> 00:06:06.733
Kind of distinguishing that
by making the actual message

139
00:06:06.733 --> 00:06:08.434
sending a send message tool call.

140
00:06:10.169 --> 00:06:10.803
And then we can

141
00:06:10.803 --> 00:06:11.471
also see

142
00:06:11.471 --> 00:06:13.106
the in-context memory of the agent.

143
00:06:13.106 --> 00:06:15.675
So this is returning back a list of blocks.

144
00:06:15.675 --> 00:06:17.543
Which of course
have the values that we set.

145
00:06:17.543 --> 00:06:19.278
So my name is Charles.

146
00:06:19.278 --> 00:06:20.680
And then also the persona.

147
00:06:20.680 --> 00:06:20.980
And then

148
00:06:20.980 --> 00:06:22.515
you can see here that we're actually

149
00:06:22.515 --> 00:06:24.751
setting a unique ID of the block.

150
00:06:24.751 --> 00:06:28.421
So you can actually retrieve
back the data and these blocks

151
00:06:28.421 --> 00:06:31.424
or even modify the blocks
by using this unique ID.

152
00:06:31.824 --> 00:06:34.927
If we want to search things like the
external message, history of the agent,

153
00:06:35.361 --> 00:06:38.197
or like the archival memories,
then we do need to go through the client.

154
00:06:38.197 --> 00:06:41.401
So for example, we can list the messages.

155
00:06:42.668 --> 00:06:43.870
So this is the full message

156
00:06:43.870 --> 00:06:44.904
history of the agent.

157
00:06:44.904 --> 00:06:46.706
So you can see that this is actually

158
00:06:46.706 --> 00:06:48.241
an initial message sequence

159
00:06:48.241 --> 00:06:50.376
that's populated into the context window

160
00:06:50.376 --> 00:06:52.745
to basically
give some examples to the agent.

161
00:06:52.745 --> 00:06:55.748
You can actually override this,
you don't want to include these.

162
00:06:55.748 --> 00:06:58.284
And then we can also see the message
that we just sent to the agent.

163
00:06:58.284 --> 00:07:01.120
"How's it going?"
The response that we get. Back, etc...

164
00:07:01.120 --> 00:07:01.888
So as this

165
00:07:01.888 --> 00:07:04.023
grows,
you'll be able to list these messages out

166
00:07:04.023 --> 00:07:05.458
and then also paginate through them.

167
00:07:06.926 --> 00:07:08.161
We can also list the archival

168
00:07:08.161 --> 00:07:09.195
memory of the agent.

169
00:07:09.195 --> 00:07:10.730
So to do this we can just

170
00:07:10.730 --> 00:07:12.331
just List the passages. Passages

171
00:07:12.331 --> 00:07:14.300
are each of the rows
in the archival memory.

172
00:07:14.300 --> 00:07:16.469
So right now
this agent has no archival memory.

173
00:07:16.469 --> 00:07:17.437
So this is just empty.

174
00:07:17.437 --> 00:07:20.139
But we'll modify this later.

175
00:07:20.139 --> 00:07:22.775
So we're now going to dive a little deeper
into core memory.

176
00:07:22.775 --> 00:07:25.778
So core memory
is the in-context memory of the agent.

177
00:07:26.078 --> 00:07:30.716
So the agent can modify memories that it
has that are stored in the context window.

178
00:07:31.117 --> 00:07:34.987
For things that might be about the human
or the persona or other memory blocks

179
00:07:34.987 --> 00:07:35.955
that you create.

180
00:07:35.955 --> 00:07:36.589
So this is

181
00:07:36.589 --> 00:07:40.159
kind of unique because it allows the agent
to essentially learn by writing

182
00:07:40.159 --> 00:07:43.196
new information into its context,
by editing a memory block.

183
00:07:44.096 --> 00:07:45.298
We can see an example of this

184
00:07:45.298 --> 00:07:48.734
by Sending a message to the agent
that gives it new information.

185
00:07:48.734 --> 00:07:49.535
So for example,

186
00:07:49.535 --> 00:07:53.473
we can tell the agent, you know, "hey,
my name is actually Sarah" versus right now

187
00:07:53.473 --> 00:07:57.343
the in-context memory about the human
says that the human's name is Charles.

188
00:07:59.345 --> 00:08:00.680
So we can see the new

189
00:08:00.680 --> 00:08:02.982
messages generated and returned back to us.

190
00:08:02.982 --> 00:08:05.952
So first,
the agent reasons. The user's name is

191
00:08:05.952 --> 00:08:06.886
updated to Sarah.

192
00:08:06.886 --> 00:08:09.021
So it's ready to engage.

193
00:08:09.021 --> 00:08:10.857
It then calls core memory replace.

194
00:08:10.857 --> 00:08:14.594
So this is one of the tools that we add
into Letta agents by default.

195
00:08:15.161 --> 00:08:18.231
And so decides for the block
with the label human

196
00:08:18.231 --> 00:08:21.501
to replace the old content,
Charles, with the new content

197
00:08:21.501 --> 00:08:22.401
Sarah.

198
00:08:22.401 --> 00:08:24.537
And then it also makes
a heartbeat request.

199
00:08:24.537 --> 00:08:26.906
So this is a special keyword argument.

200
00:08:26.906 --> 00:08:29.842
That's inserted into all Letta
tools to allow the

201
00:08:29.842 --> 00:08:32.912
LLM to control whether or not
the agent gets invoked again.

202
00:08:32.912 --> 00:08:35.014
So whether or not we run another step.

203
00:08:35.014 --> 00:08:37.316
So if this was false
then we would just stop here.

204
00:08:37.316 --> 00:08:40.286
But because it's true.
The agent gets in both again.

205
00:08:40.286 --> 00:08:42.922
And on the second invocation
it reason's ready to interact

206
00:08:42.922 --> 00:08:43.723
with Sarah, excited

207
00:08:43.723 --> 00:08:47.894
to get to know her, and then sends
a message saying: "Nice to meet you Sarah.

208
00:08:47.894 --> 00:08:48.895
What's on your mind today?"

209
00:08:50.296 --> 00:08:50.830
So if

210
00:08:50.830 --> 00:08:53.833
we actually print back out the usage
this time, we'll see that

211
00:08:53.933 --> 00:08:56.802
the step count is actually two
because of this request heartbeat.

212
00:08:56.802 --> 00:08:59.805
So the agent did two things
as opposed to doing just one thing.

213
00:09:01.240 --> 00:09:03.342
We can also retrieve this block and

214
00:09:03.342 --> 00:09:04.577
see the new value.

215
00:09:04.577 --> 00:09:05.011
So we

216
00:09:05.011 --> 00:09:07.280
can see
now that the value says my name is

217
00:09:07.280 --> 00:09:08.948
Sarah as opposed to before it

218
00:09:08.948 --> 00:09:11.317
said the name is Charles.

219
00:09:11.317 --> 00:09:13.519
Letta agents also have built-in memory

220
00:09:13.519 --> 00:09:16.822
that's not just in context memory,
but also externally stored memory.

221
00:09:16.822 --> 00:09:19.492
So we refer to this
as the archival memory.

222
00:09:19.492 --> 00:09:22.962
This is information that maybe isn't worth
putting into the context window,

223
00:09:22.962 --> 00:09:24.797
or it doesn't fit into the context window.

224
00:09:24.797 --> 00:09:28.367
And so the agent is given the ability
to insert into an external vector

225
00:09:28.367 --> 00:09:31.070
database and later
retrieve information if it needs it.

226
00:09:32.405 --> 00:09:33.973
So the agent itself has the

227
00:09:33.973 --> 00:09:38.177
ability to write to archival memory by
calling the Archival Memory insert tool.

228
00:09:38.411 --> 00:09:41.047
So we can actually just try
to explicitly trigger this

229
00:09:41.047 --> 00:09:43.316
by telling the agent
to save the information

230
00:09:43.316 --> 00:09:46.319
"Bob loves cats" into archival memory.

231
00:09:46.552 --> 00:09:48.220
And so we can see here
this kind of triggered

232
00:09:48.220 --> 00:09:48.654
the agent

233
00:09:48.654 --> 00:09:52.792
to call archival memory insert and insert
this information Bob's love of cats.

234
00:09:52.792 --> 00:09:54.060
And then it returns back

235
00:09:54.060 --> 00:09:56.062
request heartbeat is true. As well

236
00:09:56.062 --> 00:09:58.898
and so then the agent is called again
and basically sends us a message.

237
00:09:58.898 --> 00:10:01.901
I've saved the information
and Bob loves cats.

238
00:10:02.468 --> 00:10:04.337
And so now if we can actually see that

239
00:10:04.337 --> 00:10:07.440
when we list passages and get the text,
we get the text

240
00:10:07.440 --> 00:10:08.341
Bob loves cats.

241
00:10:08.341 --> 00:10:10.910
So now this information is saved
into our archival memory.

242
00:10:12.211 --> 00:10:13.846
We can also explicitly

243
00:10:13.846 --> 00:10:16.282
create archival memories as a developer.

244
00:10:16.282 --> 00:10:19.986
So if we want to insert some information
into the agent's archival memory,

245
00:10:20.186 --> 00:10:23.823
we can basically call passages dot
create with the client insert

246
00:10:23.990 --> 00:10:28.160
something like Bob Loves Boston Terriers
into the agent's archival memory.

247
00:10:29.061 --> 00:10:31.998
And so this is returning back
a bunch of information which includes,

248
00:10:31.998 --> 00:10:35.001
like the embedding generated
for the passage just inserted.

249
00:10:36.002 --> 00:10:36.936
So now that we have a

250
00:10:36.936 --> 00:10:40.973
couple memory saved into our archival,
we can see how the agent can use this.

251
00:10:41.307 --> 00:10:44.910
So I'm actually going to explicitly
trigger the agent to call archival

252
00:10:44.910 --> 00:10:46.245
by asking it a question

253
00:10:46.245 --> 00:10:47.947
like what animals do I like?

254
00:10:47.947 --> 00:10:49.348
Kind of hinting search archival.

255
00:10:49.348 --> 00:10:53.386
So, in general, the agent can itself decide
when it needs to search archival.

256
00:10:53.419 --> 00:10:57.189
But here, just for reliability, I'm going
to tell it explicitly to please search it.

257
00:10:57.890 --> 00:10:59.025
So, we can run this.

258
00:11:01.093 --> 00:11:01.661
And so we can

259
00:11:01.661 --> 00:11:04.830
see that the agent reasons user
wants to know their animal preferences.

260
00:11:04.830 --> 00:11:06.465
So it decides to search.

261
00:11:06.465 --> 00:11:08.000
It calls archival memory search.

262
00:11:08.000 --> 00:11:10.703
And it specifies the query animals.

263
00:11:10.703 --> 00:11:13.172
So the agent actually gets to decide
what the query

264
00:11:13.172 --> 00:11:16.042
that goes into archival memory search is.

265
00:11:16.042 --> 00:11:18.844
So in this case, it decided to query
the word animals.

266
00:11:18.844 --> 00:11:20.613
it can actually also
be a sentence or phrase.

267
00:11:20.613 --> 00:11:22.682
But here it was just one word.

268
00:11:22.682 --> 00:11:25.551
The agent actually also has the ability
to page through results.

269
00:11:25.551 --> 00:11:28.587
So sometimes,
there can be a very long list of results

270
00:11:28.587 --> 00:11:30.990
in an archival memory search,
in which case,

271
00:11:30.990 --> 00:11:34.427
the agent might need a page
multiple times to see all the results.

272
00:11:35.061 --> 00:11:38.764
In this case, that's not really relevant,
since we actually only have two memories

273
00:11:38.764 --> 00:11:41.067
inside of archival memory.
So it returns back

274
00:11:41.067 --> 00:11:42.768
both archival memory memories.

275
00:11:42.768 --> 00:11:45.771
So Bob loves cats,
and then also Bob loves Boston terrier.

276
00:11:45.771 --> 00:11:47.239
So this is both

277
00:11:47.239 --> 00:11:49.675
a memory
that the agent explicitly saved

278
00:11:49.675 --> 00:11:52.478
and then also the one
that we saved into its memory.

279
00:11:52.478 --> 00:11:53.245
So now it

280
00:11:53.245 --> 00:11:55.581
gets this information
into the context window

281
00:11:55.581 --> 00:11:59.318
and is able to say back, you like cats
in Boston terrier is based off

282
00:11:59.318 --> 00:12:01.454
or was in archival memory.

283
00:12:01.454 --> 00:12:04.657
So congratulations, you've
now created your first Letta agent,

284
00:12:04.657 --> 00:12:08.160
and then also learned what's
actually contained inside of the agent

285
00:12:08.160 --> 00:12:11.864
state of a Letta agent. In future
lessons will add more capabilities

286
00:12:11.864 --> 00:12:15.101
things like custom tool calling
and also customizing the in

287
00:12:15.134 --> 00:12:16.302
context memory management.

